   "text": "Read only the page below. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse. Answer each question from the page alone. When the page does not say, answer \"Not stated\".\n\n<<<PAGE\n# release-notes\n\n## What it does\n\nDrafts release notes from merged PRs between two tags. It groups PRs by Conventional Commit type, with features, fixes, and breaking changes first.\n\n## When to use it\n\nUse `release-notes` to gather merged PRs and create a release-note draft. Use `technical-writing` to edit prose.\n\nRun it manually:\n\n```text\n/release-notes <from-tag> <to-tag>\n```\n\nBoth tags must exist, and `gh` must be signed in.\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Checks authentication with `gh auth status`. If the check fails, it stops.\n2. Resolves both tags with `git rev-list -n1 <tag>`. If either tag does not exist, it stops before drafting with `TAG_UNRESOLVED`.\n3. Fetches merged PRs between the tags and skips PRs labeled `skip-notes`.\n4. Groups PRs by type. For PRs with multiple type labels, it counts labels using a strict-majority rule. The code breaks ties by label order.\n5. Checks breaking changes for a migration line. If the PR body has none, it marks the draft `TODO: migration`.\n\n## What you get\n\nA `RELEASE_NOTES.md` file in the working tree, with missing migration instructions marked for completion.\n\nThe skill never publishes. It writes the draft and stops.\nPAGE>>>\n\nQuestions:\n(a) Will it publish a release?\n(b) How do you run it?\n(c) What happens to a breaking change with no migration note?\n\nReply with only JSON: {\"a\": \"...\", \"b\": \"...\", \"c\": \"...\"}. Start the answer to (a) with Yes, No, or Not stated.",
